<h2>The revisional scheme in outline</h2>
<p>Sections 438 to 442 of the BNSS carry forward the CrPC's supervisory architecture. The High Court or Sessions Judge may call for and examine the record of any proceeding before an inferior criminal court to satisfy itself as to the correctness, legality or propriety of a finding, sentence or order, and as to the regularity of the proceedings. Two in-built limits define its character: no revision lies against an interlocutory order, and the power is discretionary supervision, not a second appeal on facts. Section 442 then clothes the High Court with the power to dispose of the revision, with safeguards — no enhancement of sentence without opportunity, no conversion of acquittal into conviction.</p>

<h2>The bar, precisely stated</h2>
<div class="compare">
  <div class="col old"><h4>Concurrent jurisdiction</h4><p>Both the Sessions Judge and the High Court can revise a magistrate's order. The applicant picks one.</p></div>
  <div class="arrow">→</div>
  <div class="col new"><h4>Section 438(3): one application per person</h4><p>Once "any person" has applied to either forum, "no further application by the same person shall be entertained by the other of them." The election, once made, exhausts the remedy.</p></div>
</div>
<p>The provision targets the applicant, not the order. The same order can face a Sessions revision by the accused and a High Court revision by the complainant, because they are not "the same person". What the statute forbids is serial revisions by one litigant working up the ladder.</p>

<h2>Why Parliament barred the second round</h2>
<div class="check">
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Finality in interlocutory supervision:</strong> revisions typically attack mid-trial orders; two tiers of revision on each would stall trials indefinitely.</li>
    <li><strong>Forum discipline:</strong> concurrent jurisdiction invites forum shopping; the one-application rule makes the choice meaningful and final.</li>
    <li><strong>Docket protection:</strong> the High Court's revisional docket is reserved for cases that come to it first or raise matters its extraordinary powers genuinely require.</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<h2>The Section 528 escape — and its honest limits</h2>
<p>Section 528 BNSS preserves the High Court's inherent powers to give effect to orders under the Sanhita, to prevent abuse of the process of any court, and to secure the ends of justice. A litigant who lost a Sessions revision often reframes the grievance as a Section 528 petition. The settled approach treats such petitions with caution: the inherent power cannot be deployed as a disguised second revision to re-appreciate the same material, but it remains available where the order under challenge discloses a jurisdictional defect, a manifest error producing a miscarriage of justice, or an abuse of process — the categories for which the power exists. In parallel, the High Court\'s constitutional supervisory jurisdiction under Article 227 polices subordinate courts on the same narrow terms. The practical meaning: the second bite exists only for the exceptional case, and petitions that merely re-argue the first revision are dismissed at the threshold.</p>

<h2>Choosing the first forum: a working matrix</h2>
<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Situation</th><th>Sensible first forum</th><th>Why</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Fact-heavy challenge (maintenance quantum, summoning on disputed material)</td><td>Sessions Judge</td><td>Quick, local, full record examined; preserves the High Court for the rare later need</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Pure question of law, or conflicting trial-court practice</td><td>High Court</td><td>Authoritative ruling; avoids a predictable second journey</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Order intertwined with a quashing case under Section 528</td><td>High Court</td><td>Composite consideration in one forum avoids splitting the challenge</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Urgency with custody consequences</td><td>Forum that can list fastest in the circumstances</td><td>Interim protection matters more than doctrinal preference</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>Common traps in practice</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>The casual Sessions revision</strong> — filed as a reflex, argued thinly, dismissed — and the better case now barred before the High Court. File once, file fully.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>The mislabelled petition</strong> — styling a second revision as a 528 petition changes nothing; courts look at substance, and the bar applies to what the petition really is.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Forgetting the other side's rights</strong> — the opponent, unbarred, may revise in the other forum; anticipate and be ready to respond there.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Reviving via fresh orders</strong> — a genuinely new order passed later in the proceedings gives rise to a fresh revisional right; the bar attaches per application and order, not to the case forever.</div>
</div>
<div class="note">
  <p>This article explains the statutory scheme in general terms, using the BNSS provisions with their CrPC lineage. It is information, not advice on any pending matter.</p>
</div>
